    <briefdescription>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>Implements cache data members. <ref refid="classA" kindref="compound">A</ref> cache data member either stores a value or stores no value. When its value is present, it can be obtained, and when absent it has no value (not even a default constructed value). The value of a cache data member can be modified even when its object is const. For instance, an object that caches an expensive-to-compute value could be declared thusly:</para><para><programlisting><codeline><highlight class="normal">struct<sp/>Foo<sp/>{</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/>Cached&lt;Bar&gt;<sp/>bar_;</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal">};</highlight></codeline>
</programlisting></para><para>When a Foo object is constructed the <computeroutput>bar_</computeroutput> member will contain nothing (not even a default-constructed object). Here&apos;s a sample implementation that returns the value, performing the expensive calculation and caching the result if necessary. The method is declared to take a const <computeroutput>this</computeroutput> pointer since it is conceptually a data member accessor:</para><para><programlisting><codeline><highlight class="normal">const<sp/>Bar&amp;<sp/>Foo::bar()<sp/>const<sp/>{<sp/>//typical<sp/>accessor<sp/>declaration</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/>if<sp/>(!bar_.isCached())</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/>bar_<sp/>=<sp/>expensiveComputation();</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/>return<sp/>bar_.get();</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal">}</highlight></codeline>
</programlisting> </para>    </detaileddescription>
